<section class="about" aria-labelledby="how">
  <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <h3>Distance to the edge</h3>
      <p>For each pixel of the card, the code measures how far it sits inside the rounded rectangle. Past the bezel width nothing moves, so the text area stays sharp.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Shift inward</h3>
      <p>Near the rim, each pixel takes its colour from a point further inside the card. The shift grows with the square of closeness to the edge, which reads as a curved glass lip.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Split the colours</h3>
      <p>Red is shifted a little more than green and blue a little less. That gives the faint prism fringe real glass shows at its edge.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Tint and rim light</h3>
      <p>A dark gradient keeps white text readable, and a 1px gradient border adds the highlight along the top and bottom edges.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="limits">
    <h3>Scope</h3>
    <p>
      The glass refracts the image you give it, which fits stat cards and captions over hero photos. Bending live page content
      (text, video, scrolling sections) needs an SVG <code>backdrop-filter</code>, which only Chromium browsers support today.
      Web output uses Canvas 2D; Flutter uses a fragment shader; SwiftUI uses a Metal <code>layerEffect</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

